//@version=5
// V15 -- the synthesis, and the correction that came with building it.
//
// ================================ WHAT THIS IS ================================
// A SHORT book on the 07:00-11:00 New York window, entered with a RESTING LIMIT, run on US30 and
// US100 together. An optional long leg is included and is OFF, with its numbers shown.
//
//   SHORT (default on)   Donchian 30 down-break | EMA13 < EMA34 | ADX(14) >= 22
//                        entry: resting limit 0.75 x ATR(5) ABOVE the close, 8-bar expiry
//                        stop 2.5 x ATR(20) or the 25-bar high, whichever is NEARER | target 2R
//                        entries 07:00-11:00 New York only; exits run free
//   LONG  (default off)  Donchian 30 up-break | ADX >= 25 AND efficiency(20) >= 0.30 AND chop(14) <= 55
//                        AND EMA13 > EMA48 AND EMA12 > EMA100 | limit 0.75 x ATR(5) BELOW
//                        stop 2.0N or the 20-bar low | NO target | all hours
//
// Points are not comparable across two instruments, so every trade is divided by its own stop
// distance and the book is measured in R.
//
// ================================ READ THIS BEFORE THE NUMBERS ================================
// **THE FIGURES BELOW ARE HALF WHAT THE RESEARCH ENGINE REPORTED, AND THE ENGINE WAS WRONG.**
// The parity harness (research/v15/v15_parity.py) re-implements this script's order model and
// diffs it against the engine. Every trade the two share is IDENTICAL -- exit bar 100%, P&L
// correlation 1.0000 on all four legs. They do not share all their trades:
//
//                        engine                 this script            kept
//   US30  short       216 trades  +62.4R      174 trades  +29.5R        47%
//   US30  long        311 trades  +58.6R      260 trades  +13.9R        24%
//   US100 short       214 trades  +55.9R      168 trades  +17.5R        31%
//   US100 long        276 trades  +97.0R      246 trades  +34.0R        35%
//
// The engine scans forward from each signal in turn and fills at THAT signal's level, so a limit
// set eight bars ago outranks a nearer one set since. Reproducing that needs eight simultaneous
// resting orders AND for the far one to fill first, which is not how a book fills. A script has
// ONE live order. Every number below is computed with the order model this script can actually
// run. The same correction applies to every limit-entry figure in docs/ib/STUDY_V10_LIMIT.md and
// docs/ib/STUDY_V14_WINDOW_GRID.md -- see docs/ib/STUDY_V15_BOOK.md.
//
// ================================ SELECTED ON TRAIN, JUDGED ONCE ================================
// Train 2024-08 to 2025-12. The control draws random entries with the SAME side, geometry, order
// type and minute-of-day mix, 300 times per leg, and asks how often it beats the rule.
//
//   book             train R  Sharpe  maxDD   ctrl p  |  2026 R  Sharpe  maxDD  ret/DD
//   shorts, LIMIT      +24.0    1.32   13.2    0.003  |   +23.0    2.50    9.2    2.50   <-- SHIPPED
//   shorts, market      +5.5    0.20   26.2    0.330  |   +22.9    1.82   13.3    1.72
//   full,   LIMIT      +66.3    1.26   33.0    0.017  |   +28.6    1.14   24.9    1.15
//   full,   market     +59.9    0.89   31.2    0.357  |   +42.4    1.32   29.2    1.45
//
// The choice was made on the train column and the control. **The limit is what beats the control;
// with market orders neither book does (p 0.33, p 0.36).** Adding the long leg nearly triples the
// judged drawdown, 9.2R to 24.9R, and halves Sharpe, which is why it ships off.
//
// The judged block, 2026-01-01 to 2026-08-26, was read ONCE: 81 trading days, +23.0R, Sharpe 2.50,
// max drawdown 9.22R. Against the same matched control, **p = 0.010** -- and the control's MEDIAN
// outcome is -10.9R, so random entries with this geometry LOSE. The rule is doing the work.
//
// ================================ WHAT THE MECHANIC IS AND IS NOT ================================
// An earlier reading of this data said the limit beat a market order in all eight cells. With the
// implementable order model it does not -- it wins four and loses four:
//
//                        market order        resting limit
//   US30  short train    PF 0.84  -18.5      PF 1.23  +15.6      limit
//   US30  short judge    PF 1.12   +6.4      PF 1.50  +14.0      limit
//   US30  long  train    PF 1.18  +25.0      PF 1.29  +30.1      limit
//   US30  long  judge    PF 1.05   +3.4      PF 0.70  -16.1      market
//   US100 short train    PF 1.23  +24.0      PF 1.13   +8.4      market
//   US100 short judge    PF 1.30  +16.6      PF 1.29   +9.1      tie
//   US100 long  train    PF 1.25  +29.4      PF 1.14  +12.3      market
//   US100 long  judge    PF 1.27  +16.0      PF 1.47  +21.7      limit
//
// So the honest claim is narrower than the one it replaces: the limit is not uniformly the better
// fill, but it is the version that clears a matched control, and it does so by taking FEWER and
// better trades. That is also why it survives being judged: on 2026 the two mechanics earn the
// same net R (+23.0 against +22.9) and the limit does it at Sharpe 2.50 against 1.82, on half the
// drawdown.
//
// ================================ STABILITY ================================
//   Quarters, both instruments, 2024-Q3 to 2026-Q3, in R:
//     -0.9  +2.5  +10.4  +4.3  -0.5  +8.2  +19.8  +4.3  -1.1     6 of 9 positive, worst -1.1R
//   Monte Carlo, 20,000 shuffles of the judged daily series: realised drawdown 9.22R against a
//     median of 9.4 and a p99 of 18.3. **SIZE FOR 18R, NOT THE 9 YOU CAN SEE.**
//   Bootstrap on the judged block: P(mean daily R <= 0) = 0.073. That is NOT significant at 5%.
//     The rule clears its control (p 0.010); it does not clear zero on 81 days of daily data.
//     Eight months is a short holdout and this is what a short holdout can and cannot say.
//   Drop a random 10% of days: Sharpe p5 1.47, median 2.48. No single stretch carries it.
//
// ================================ POSITION SIZING ================================
// Risk a FIXED FRACTION per trade, sized off the stop distance, never a fixed contract count.
// 60 trading days, 6% target, 4% trailing drawdown, on the shipped book:
//
//   risk/trade   P(pass)   P(bust)   edge     P(neither)
//      0.25%      25.5%     12.8%   +12.8%      61.7%    <-- best edge
//      0.50%      49.4%     47.9%    +1.5%       2.7%
//      0.75%      45.3%     54.7%    -9.4%       0.0%
//      1.00%      42.3%     57.7%   -15.5%       0.0%
//
// Read the last column before the first. **At the only risk level with a real edge, the likeliest
// outcome by far is that 60 days ends with neither a pass nor a bust.** This book does not clear a
// 6% target inside an evaluation window at survivable risk; sized to survive it simply grinds.
// Treat it as an income book that can be run on a funded account, not as an evaluation strategy.
//
// ================================ THE LIMITATION THAT GATES ALL OF IT ================================
// **THIS RESTS ON A LIMIT ENTRY AND COULD NOT BE SETTLED AT 1-MINUTE RESOLUTION.**
// docs/ib/STUDY_V10_LIMIT.md showed a limit-entry backtest run on the same bars that decide the
// exits measures intrabar ordering, and that three such artifacts together were worth a Sharpe of
// 11 on a rule-free test. This model carries all three fixes -- on the fill bar only the ATR stop
// is live, no target and no channel -- and the standing rule on this branch is that such questions
// are settled on the TRUE 1-MINUTE PATH. Only 15-minute bars exist for US30 and US100, so that
// test could not be run, and the order-model correction above is exactly the class of error that
// hides at this resolution.
// **One-minute US30 and US100 data is the single highest-value thing that could be added.**
//
// A one-bar scalp is arithmetically dead here regardless: 180 IC tests put the largest |IC| at
// 0.0305, where h=1 needs |IC| >= 0.10 to clear a 1.2-point round turn. Holds here are hours.
// ==========================================================================================

strategy("V15 book -- 07:00-11:00 short, limit entry", overlay = true, pyramiding = 0,
     default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1,
     initial_capital = 100000, calc_on_every_tick = false, process_orders_on_close = false,
     commission_type = strategy.commission.cash_per_order, commission_value = 0.72, slippage = 1)

// ===================== STRATEGY TESTER PROPERTIES =====================
//   Script execution   ON BAR CLOSE only -- see docs/ib/STUDY_TICK_RECALC.md.
//   Bar detalization   HIGH, WITH DEEP BACKTESTING. Not optional: the whole question is whether the
//                      limit filled before the stop, and without intrabar data the emulator guesses.
//   Slippage           1 tick. A resting limit pays none on entry -- you provide liquidity -- but
//                      the exit stop does.
//   Leverage Infinity  stops silent order rejection; then read DOLLAR drawdown, not %.
//   Run it on US30 AND US100 together. The book's numbers are the two markets combined.
// ======================================================================

gS       = "Short leg -- the shipped book"
useShort = input.bool(true, "Trade the SHORT leg", group = gS)
winStart = input.int(420, "Entry window start, minutes after New York midnight", minval=0, maxval=1439, group=gS)
winEnd   = input.int(660, "Entry window end", minval=0, maxval=1440, group=gS,
     tooltip="420-660 = 07:00-11:00 New York. Entries only; once open, the trade exits on its own stop and channel however long that takes.")
sDon     = input.int(30,  "Donchian entry length", minval=2, group=gS)
sFast    = input.int(13,  "EMA fast", minval=2, group=gS)
sSlow    = input.int(34,  "EMA slow", minval=3, group=gS)
sAdx     = input.float(22,"ADX(14) floor", minval=0, step=1, group=gS)
sStop    = input.float(2.5,"ATR stop multiple", minval=0.1, step=0.1, group=gS)
sTp      = input.float(2.0,"Target, in R (0 = none)", minval=0, step=0.5, group=gS)
sExit    = input.int(25,  "Donchian exit channel", minval=2, group=gS)

gL       = "Long leg -- OFF: it nearly triples the book's drawdown out of sample"
useLong  = input.bool(false,"Trade the LONG leg", group = gL,
     tooltip="On the 2026 judge block adding it took net R from 23.0 to 28.6 and max drawdown from 9.2R to 24.9R, cutting Sharpe 2.50 -> 1.14 and return/drawdown 2.50 -> 1.15.")
lDon     = input.int(30,  "Donchian entry length", minval=2, group=gL)
lStop    = input.float(2.0,"ATR stop multiple", minval=0.1, step=0.1, group=gL)
lExit    = input.int(20,  "Donchian exit channel", minval=2, group=gL)
lAdx     = input.float(25,"ADX floor", minval=0, step=1, group=gL)
lEr      = input.float(0.30,"Efficiency ratio(20) floor", minval=0, step=0.05, group=gL)
lChop    = input.float(55,"Choppiness(14) ceiling", minval=0, step=1, group=gL)

gE       = "Entry mechanic -- what makes the book clear its matched control"
limMult  = input.float(0.75,"Resting limit, x ATR(5) in our favour", minval=0, step=0.05, group=gE,
     tooltip="Set to 0 for market orders. The short book then fails a matched control at p 0.33 on train, and its judged Sharpe falls 2.50 -> 1.82 on twice the drawdown.")
limWait  = input.int(8,   "Bars the limit rests before cancelling", minval=1, group=gE)
qty      = input.int(1,   "Contracts (size by RISK, not by count -- see the header)", minval=1, group=gE)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- series
// ta.atr IS Wilder's RMA of true range. The LIMIT distance uses ema(TR,5), a DIFFERENT series --
// that is what the research layer uses for it, and swapping them moves every level in the script.
atrN   = ta.atr(20)
atrLim = ta.ema(ta.tr(true), 5)
sLo    = ta.lowest(low,  sDon)[1]          // ENTRY channel: excludes this bar so a break is possible
lHi    = ta.highest(high, lDon)[1]
// EXIT channels take NO [1]. The order is placed at this bar's close and is live on the NEXT bar,
// so `ta.highest(high, n)` here is the same window the engine reads on the bar it fires. Writing
// [1] -- which looks right beside the entry channels -- makes every exit one bar staler than the
// research and was worth a fifth of the trades in the parity diff.
sHi    = ta.highest(high, sExit)
lLo    = ta.lowest(low,  lExit)
[_pdi, _mdi, adxVal] = ta.dmi(14, 14)      // returns [+DI, -DI, ADX] IN THAT ORDER
eF     = ta.ema(close, sFast)
eS     = ta.ema(close, sSlow)
e13    = ta.ema(close, 13)
e48    = ta.ema(close, 48)
e12    = ta.ema(close, 12)
e100   = ta.ema(close, 100)
netMv  = math.abs(close - close[20])
totMv  = math.sum(math.abs(close - close[1]), 20)
effRat = totMv > 0 ? netMv / totMv : na
sumTr  = math.sum(ta.tr(true), 14)
rng14  = ta.highest(high, 14) - ta.lowest(low, 14)
chop14 = rng14 > 0 and sumTr > 0 ? 100 * math.log10(sumTr / rng14) / math.log10(14) : na

// BARE `hour` AND `minute` ARE EXCHANGE TIME IN PINE -- Chicago for CME. Read New York explicitly.
modNY  = hour(time, "America/New_York") * 60 + minute(time, "America/New_York")
inWin  = modNY >= winStart and modNY < winEnd
ready  = not na(atrN) and atrN > 0 and not na(atrLim) and atrLim > 0

okShort = useShort and ready and inWin and (not na(adxVal) and adxVal >= sAdx) and eF < eS
     and not na(sLo) and low < sLo
regimeL = (not na(adxVal) and adxVal >= lAdx) and (not na(effRat) and effRat >= lEr)
     and (not na(chop14) and chop14 <= lChop)
okLong  = useLong and ready and regimeL and e13 > e48 and e12 > e100 and not na(lHi) and high > lHi

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- state
var float sigAtr   = na      // ATR FROZEN AT THE SIGNAL BAR; stop and target read this all trade
var int   pendBar  = na
var int   pendSide = 0
var int   sideOn   = 0
var int   lastCnt  = 0
var int   closedBar = na     // the bar a position went flat on -- no re-arming on it

// EVERY var-STATE BLOCK IS GUARDED, AND SO IS THE EXIT CALL -- docs/ib/STUDY_TICK_RECALC.md.
if barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.position_size == 0 and lastCnt > 0
    sigAtr := na
    sideOn := 0
    lastCnt := 0
    closedBar := bar_index

if barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.opentrades > lastCnt
    sideOn := pendSide
    pendBar := na
    pendSide := 0
    lastCnt := strategy.opentrades

flat = strategy.position_size == 0 and (na(closedBar) or bar_index > closedBar)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- entry
// ONE LIVE ORDER, AND IT IS NOT RE-PRICED. While the limit rests, new signals are IGNORED; at
// expiry it is cancelled and the bar it was cancelled on may arm a new one. Re-pricing on every
// fresh signal is the obvious alternative and it is strictly worse -- it chases the market and
// gives back most of the leg (research/v15/v15_parity.py, arm = "replace").
// The order price and the ATR are BOTH frozen at the bar the order is placed. The bracket rides
// with the entry so the FILL BAR is covered: `loss` is measured by Pine from the ACTUAL fill.
if barstate.isconfirmed and flat
    if not na(pendBar) and bar_index - pendBar >= limWait
        strategy.cancel_all()
        pendBar := na
        pendSide := 0
    if na(pendBar)
        if okShort
            sigAtr := atrN
            pendBar := bar_index
            pendSide := -1
            lTk = int(math.max(1, math.round(sStop * atrN / syminfo.mintick)))
            px = limMult <= 0 ? na : close + limMult * atrLim
            if na(px)
                strategy.entry("S", strategy.short, qty = qty)
            else
                strategy.entry("S", strategy.short, qty = qty, limit = px)
            // NO `profit` HERE. On the fill bar the limit filled because the bar traded UP to it;
            // paying that same bar's low for the 2R target assumes the high came first and the low
            // after -- one bar, both extremes, in our favour. That single assumption was worth a
            // Sharpe of 11 on a rule-free test. The target starts on the NEXT bar, with "x".
            strategy.exit("x0", from_entry = "S", loss = lTk)
        else if okLong
            sigAtr := atrN
            pendBar := bar_index
            pendSide := 1
            lTk = int(math.max(1, math.round(lStop * atrN / syminfo.mintick)))
            px = limMult <= 0 ? na : close - limMult * atrLim
            if na(px)
                strategy.entry("L", strategy.long, qty = qty)
            else
                strategy.entry("L", strategy.long, qty = qty, limit = px)
            strategy.exit("x0", from_entry = "L", loss = lTk)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- exit
// Working stop = the NEARER of the ATR stop and the exit channel, then CAPPED AT THIS BAR'S CLOSE.
// A sell stop cannot rest above the market and a buy stop cannot rest below it. Left uncapped,
// Pine triggers such an order at the next bar's OPEN, which books a gap the research never gave
// itself; capping at the close of the bar the order is placed on is what the engine does.
avg      = strategy.position_avg_price
stopS    = na(avg) or na(sigAtr) ? na : avg + sStop * sigAtr
stopL    = na(avg) or na(sigAtr) ? na : avg - lStop * sigAtr
lvlS0    = na(stopS) ? sHi : (na(sHi) ? stopS : math.min(stopS, sHi))
lvlL0    = na(stopL) ? lLo : (na(lLo) ? stopL : math.max(stopL, lLo))
lvlS     = na(lvlS0) ? na : math.max(lvlS0, close)
lvlL     = na(lvlL0) ? na : math.min(lvlL0, close)
exitLvl  = sideOn < 0 ? lvlS : lvlL
tpLvl    = sideOn < 0 and sTp > 0 and not na(avg) and not na(sigAtr) ? avg - sTp * sStop * sigAtr : na

if barstate.isconfirmed and not flat and lastCnt > 0 and not na(exitLvl)
    // "x0" covered the fill bar; "x" takes over. Cancel the first or both rest.
    strategy.cancel("x0")
    strategy.exit("x", stop = exitLvl, limit = tpLvl)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- plots
plot(useShort ? sLo : na, "Donchian 30 low", color = color.new(color.aqua, 35))
plot(useLong  ? lHi : na, "Donchian 30 high", color = color.new(color.teal, 45))
plot(flat and not na(pendBar) and pendSide < 0 ? close + limMult * atrLim : na, "Resting limit",
     color = color.new(color.orange, 0), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2)
plot(flat and not na(pendBar) and pendSide > 0 ? close - limMult * atrLim : na, "Resting limit",
     color = color.new(color.orange, 0), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 2)
plot(strategy.position_size == 0 ? na : exitLvl, "Working stop", color = color.fuchsia, style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(strategy.position_size == 0 ? na : tpLvl,   "Target",       color = color.new(color.lime, 0), style = plot.style_linebr)
bgcolor(inWin and useShort ? color.new(color.teal, 94) : na, title = "short entry window")

onTf = timeframe.period == "15"
var table hud = table.new(position.top_right, 1, 4, border_width = 1)
if barstate.islast
    table.cell(hud, 0, 0, "V15 book - 2026 Sharpe 2.50, maxDD 9.2R, control p 0.010",
         text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.teal, 20))
    table.cell(hud, 0, 1, onTf ? "15m chart" : "NOT 15m - studied on 15m only",
         text_color = color.white, bgcolor = onTf ? color.new(color.teal, 45) : color.red)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 2, "Size at 0.25% risk. MC p99 drawdown is 18R, not the 9 you see.",
         text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.orange, 20))
    table.cell(hud, 0, 3, "Rests on a LIMIT entry unsettled at 1-minute resolution. Deep Backtesting required.",
         text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.red, 20))
